Unfortunately, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text-based data interchange format that represents structured data in an easy-to-read and human-friendly way. However, JSON does not have built-in sorting functionality as it's just a data format. To sort the keys of a JSON object, you need to first convert the JSON object into a JavaScript object using `JSON.parse()`, sort the keys based on your desired sorting algorithm, and then convert the sorted JavaScript object back into a JSON string using `JSON.stringify()`. Here's a simple function that takes a JSON string as input, sorts the keys based on their length (shortest to longest), and returns the sorted JSON string:

```javascript
function sortJSON(jsonStr) {
  const obj = JSON.parse(jsonStr);
  const sortedKeys = Object.keys(obj).sort((a, b) => Math.min(obj[a].length || Infinity, obj[b].length || Infinity) - Math.max(obj[a].length || Infinity, obj[b].length || Infinity));
  const sortedObj = {};
  for (let I = 0; I < sortedKeys.length; i++) {
    sortedObj[sortedKeys[i]] = obj[sortedKeys[i]];
  }
  return JSON.stringify(sortedObj);
}
```

